WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.840
Glad you're here for unintended consequences, episode 43 for June 29th, 2026. Monday, a fresh

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:15.500
case to chew on as the week begins. Automakers recast streets as car space and turned crossing them

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:22.980
into a crime, reshaping cities for a century. In 1923, Cincinnati police began ticketing adults

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:29.040
for stepping off the curb anywhere except at a corner crosswalk. An act that had been ordinary for

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.200
generations suddenly carried a fine because the city had decided that only the intersection

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.820
counted as a legal place to enter the roadway. The change followed intensive lobbying by automobile

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:47.820
manufacturers and local motor clubs. They argued that pedestrians, not drivers, were the main source of

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.500
collisions and they backed that claim with the collision data they had collected from their own clubs.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.440
What began as an effort to reduce deaths on crowded streets ended by redefining the street

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.160
itself as a place where people on foot no longer belonged by right. In other words, the curb became

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.900
a legal boundary rather than a simple physical edge. In the early 1920s, American cities faced

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:20.040
sharply rising pedestrian fatalities. Motor vehicle registrations climbed from roughly 8 million in

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:28.460
1920 to more than 20 million by 1927. That is an increase of more than 12 million vehicles in seven

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:35.100
years which meant that on many downtown blocks the number of cars passing any given point had roughly

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:42.160
doubled or tripled within the span of a single city council term. City engineers, police officials and

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.840
insurance companies viewed unregulated street use as a public health crisis. Children still played in

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.300
roadways and adults crossed wherever they chose because nothing in the existing common law tradition

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:01.780
had ever told them they could not. The national conference on street and highway safety convened in 1924

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.600
under Secretary of Commerce Herbert Hoover brought together traffic engineers and safety advocates.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.200
They believed uniform rules could separate flows of movement and lower the death toll by making each

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:20.680
user's location predictable. Automobile trade groups such as the national automobile chamber of

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:27.200
commerce joined these meetings. They contributed data on collisions and argued that clear legal

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.400
priority for vehicles would protect everyone including the pedestrians who would now know exactly

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.620
where they were supposed to be. At the time the proposals seemed technical and neutral.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:44.120
codify where pedestrians should walk so drivers could anticipate them and the arithmetic

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:50.840
of crashes would improve. No one in those rooms imagined the rules would later be used to justify

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.580
the near total redesign of urban space around the automobile because the discussion stayed focused

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:06.580
on the single variable of mid-block collisions. Between 1924 and 1930 dozens of cities passed ordinances

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:13.460
that criminalized J-walking. The term was popularized by auto club publicity that labeled mid-block

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:20.320
crossing as rural ignorance. A rhetorical move that turned an everyday habit into a social failing.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:28.720
Los Angeles adopted one of the earliest strict statutes in 1925. New York, Chicago, and Detroit

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.160
followed within a few years each copying language that had already been tested in court.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.880
Model legislation circulated by the American Automobile Association helped spread the language

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:47.360
to smaller cities that lacked their own drafting staff. Newspapers ran safety campaigns funded

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.820
by motor interests that showed cartoon jays darting into traffic and being struck. And those

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:59.020
images appeared week after week in the same papers that also carried automobile advertising.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:05.100
School programs taught children to treat streets as dangerous territory reserved for cars,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.340
so that an entire generation learned the new boundary as if it had always existed.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.620
Early data from cities that enforced the rules showed a measurable drop in mid-block collisions.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:23.940
Proponents cited this as proof the policy worked and the drop was real on the specific metric they measured.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:29.740
A few planners and downtown merchants warned that the laws would discourage foot traffic

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.760
and favor suburban growth. Yet their objections were treated as minor compared with the visible

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.060
reduction in downtown accidents because the accident count inside city limits was the only number

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:48.820
the conference had asked anyone to watch. Once jaywalking statutes were on the books the legal balance

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:55.120
of liability shifted decisively. Courts began treating any pedestrian outside a marked crossing

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.680
as contributorily negligent which meant that even a driver traveling well above the posted speed

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.820
could escape most financial responsibility if the injured person had not been at a corner.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:13.180
This sharply reduced the number of successful lawsuits against drivers and insurers because

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:19.700
the pedestrian now carried the heavier burden of proof. With legal risk lowered cities widened

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:26.080
roadways, removed street car tracks and added traffic signals timed for vehicle throughput rather than

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:33.040
foot movement. The arithmetic here is straightforward. Once insurers paid out fewer claims per mile of

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:39.360
road municipal engineers could justify spending tax dollars to add lanes and to synchronize lights

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.720
so that a continuous stream of cars could pass through an intersection without stopping for people

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:51.560
waiting to cross. The second order effect was rapid. Retail districts that had relied on easy pedestrian

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:58.260
access lost customers to new drive and shopping centers built at the urban fringe because the same

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:04.040
widened roads that sped cars through downtown also made it practical to locate stores where parking

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:11.060
lots could be larger and cheaper. This accelerated the decentralization that later became suburban sprawl.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:17.220
By the 1950s, federal highway funding further embedded the assumption that streets existed

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:24.520
primarily for cars. Neighborhoods built before 1920 found themselves bisected by high-speed arterials

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:30.120
where crossing on foot required long detours sometimes several blocks out of the way.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:36.520
Third order consequences compounded over decades. Reduced street life lowered informal

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.700
surveillance contributing to the sense that downtowns were unsafe after dark because fewer people were

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:49.020
simply walking past storefronts at ordinary hours. Public health researchers later documented that

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:55.180
the same infrastructure changes correlated with sharp declines and routine walking. A factor now

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:02.220
linked to rising rates of obesity and cardiovascular disease. The original safety goal was never abandoned

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:08.580
yet the system that emerged produced a built environment in which the safest legal choice for most people

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:15.640
was to drive even short distances because the physical layout and the liability rules now reinforced

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:23.040
each other. By the 1970s some transportation planners began questioning whether the 1920s rules had

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:30.160
overshot their mark. Experiments with shared space designs in Europe and later in a handful of US

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:36.620
cities such as West Palm Beach and New York's Times Square showed that removing strict separation

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:42.900
could slow traffic and improve pedestrian safety without new criminal penalties. Most American

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:49.380
municipalities, however, retained the J-walking statutes. Enforcement simply became sporadic so

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:55.240
the legal rule stayed on the books while daily practice varied by neighborhood and by police department

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:03.300
priorities. Recent state-level reforms in places such as Virginia in 2020 and California in 2022

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:10.320
have decriminalized mid-block crossing when it can be done safely. Yet the physical legacy of wide lanes

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:17.060
and limited crosswalks remains expensive to undo because every added lane and every removed street

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:24.100
car track represented capital spending that cities are still amortizing. The meta-irony is that many

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:30.920
current complete streets and vision zero programs must now spend public funds to reverse design choices

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:37.160
that were themselves sold as safety measures a century earlier. When a powerful interest group

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:43.720
frames a safety problem in terms that align with its own technology the resulting rules can quietly

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:50.340
transfer rights and resources for generations. Complex urban systems rarely respond to single

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.980
variable interventions. Reassigning legal priority on streets altered land use patterns, retail

00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:03.200
economics, and daily physical activity in ways no 1924 conference table could have modeled.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:09.680
Because each change in liability or road with created new incentives that compounded across

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:16.500
decades. The durable lesson is to test any proposed reallocation of public space against the question

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:23.120
of who will still be able to move freely once the new rules are enforced and the concrete has said.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:29.780
Consider today's debates over dedicated lanes for autonomous delivery robots on city sidewalks.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.320
Who gains priority and who loses the ability to linger or cross without penalty?

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.760
That's unintended consequences for today. If this episode gave you something to think about,

